            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house is pretty good for air tightness, however there are a few areas that could be addressed:



** The weather-stripping &amp; door sweeps for exterior doors (primarily the 2 on the main floor)



** Wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls can benefit from foam backer pads &amp; having receptacles that are draft proof



** Around pipes &amp; wires that travel through outside walls



** Keeping the spaces between the logs on the main floor well sealed



Savings: The energy savings associated with a 10% reduction in air leakage is estimated to save $ 101 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.9 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

You just completed a wall insulation upgrade on the 2nd floor walls, that represent about 40% of the total exterior wall area of your house. You increased the previous R24 insulation up to R36 &amp; qualified for a $1,200 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



Although the main floor walls of your house are log &amp; most folks do not add insulation to the interior or exterior, I thought I would just illustrate the estimated savings associated with adding a 2x4 wall on the interior with an R14 Roxul batt of insulation - just for curiosity !!



Savings: By adding a minimum R14 to the interior of the main floor walls, you could potentially save $ 337 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,800 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Simpson, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 2 bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer ..... and the range hood recirculates inside the house), indicated a pressure drop of 2.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current propane boiler (heating the house &amp; your hot water) is a direct vent model &amp; is not susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, we did see smoke detectors however did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - a clothes dryer

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - the kitchen range hood fan does not vent to the outside



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.31 per kWh for the first 600 kWh, $0.72 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.27 per litre 

Propane - $0.87 per litre delivered

Wood - $315 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
